p.src:
The main structure is $\text{perception}
\longrightarrow
\text{thought}
\longrightarrow
\text{memory}
\longrightarrow
\text{repetition}
\longrightarrow
\text{pleasure as pursuit}.$ The answer is not control. Control belongs to division and suppression. The alternative is complete attention, in which seeing and action are not separated: $\text{attention}
\longrightarrow
\text{seeing and action together}.$ From this, discipline receives a different meaning: $\text{discipline}
=
\text{learning}.$ When the mind learns the structure of pleasure directly, that learning brings its own order. The inquiry then opens naturally toward attention, transformation, the sacred, and the later question of beauty.

